The prose samples in this directory are NOT covered by the repository's MIT
licence. They come from two source families, one per subdirectory:

    gutenberg/     Public domain (works out of copyright)
                   https://www.gutenberg.org/policy/permission.html
                   Attribution recorded in ATTRIBUTION.json.

    pluralistic/   Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC-BY 4.0)
                   https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
                   Cory Doctorow's stated license on every post at pluralistic.net.
                   Attribution recorded in ATTRIBUTION.modern.json.

Each subdirectory has its own attribution file. Redistribution must carry the
per-file license the file was published under: CC-BY for pluralistic content
(cite pluralistic.net + Cory Doctorow), public-domain terms for gutenberg
content.

Project Gutenberg's own Trademark License applies to any redistribution that
carries the "Project Gutenberg" name. To stay clear of it, each essay here has
its frontmatter naming Project Gutenberg as the SOURCE only, and the text body
itself carries no PG boilerplate - the ***START/END*** markers, the header and
license notice, and the footer are stripped by fetch-essays.mjs.

ATTRIBUTION.json lists every essay's author, source volume, Gutenberg eBook id,
and original publication date. The eBook id resolves to
https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/<id> for anyone re-deriving the corpus.

Why each gutenberg/ author is public domain, one line each. Two independent
grounds are given because the corpus is redistributed worldwide: publication
before the 1930 US cutoff, and author death more than 70 years ago.

    Francis Bacon        Essays 1625.                 d. 1626.
    G. K. Chesterton     Tremendous Trifles 1909.     d. 1936.
    Anton Chekhov        Garnett translation 1920.    d. 1904; Garnett d. 1946.
    Kate Chopin          The Awakening 1899.          d. 1904.
    O. Henry             The Four Million 1906.       d. 1910 (W. S. Porter).
    Thomas H. Huxley     Science & Education 1893.    d. 1895.
    Charles Darwin       Voyage of the Beagle 1839.   d. 1882.

Chekhov is the only one where the translation carries its own copyright term;
Constance Garnett died in 1946, so the 1920 translation is out of copyright on
both grounds as well.

Huxley and Darwin were added on 2026-08-06 for the EXPLANATORY register, which
none of the five above covers: essays argue, letters address, chapters narrate,
and none of them takes something the reader does not know and builds it up from
general statements. That register matters to the pattern critic's
false-positive sweep specifically, because explanatory prose is abstract and
general by nature - the surface shape of the patterns that sweep is looking
for. Both are public domain on both grounds independently.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------
THIS DIRECTORY IS SINGLE-AUTHOR. THAT IS THE POINT OF IT.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

Every subdirectory here holds work by ONE identifiable writer, with enough
samples (>=15) to characterise that writer's cadence. Voice calibration and
the voice critic's cross-author test depend on it: both assume "these samples
were written by the same person", and both produce a confident measurement of
nobody if that is false.

Multi-author collections do NOT belong here even when the licence is fine.
They live in sibling directories - `human/` (Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 4.0) and
`human-professional/` (EFF Deeplinks, CC-BY 4.0) - each with its own LICENSE.
Those exist for the same reason this one does: to give the critics real prose
to be tested against, with provenance a reader can check. They just answer a
different question, and mixing them silently would turn an authorship test
into a register test without anyone noticing.

Nothing may be added to this directory except text that is public domain or
under a licence at least as permissive - CC0, CC-BY, or an OSI-approved
equivalent - with provenance recorded the same way. Not contemporary
journalism (except CC-BY-published), not contemporary books, not private
writing, however good a sample it would make. The tests do not need it; the
public repo cannot host it.
